        "content": {
          "Prompt": {
            "prompt": "can't we just pass sizeof(arr) as the parameter to the function?"
          }
        },
        "timestamp": "2026-10-06T15:17:29.224857400+07:00",
        "images": null
      },
      "assistant": {
        "Response": {
          "message_id": "1cd230da-fb37-42a4-8aa6-a14eeaa19ed8",
          "content": "Great question — and yes, that works, but *only if you compute `sizeof` in the right place*. The trick is **where** you call it.\n\n**This works** — compute `sizeof` in `main`, where `a` is still a real array:\n\n```c\nf(a, sizeof(a) / sizeof(a[0]));   // 10, computed BEFORE decay\n```\n\nHere `sizeof(a)` is 40 and `sizeof(a[0])` is 4, so you pass `10`. Correct.\n\n**This does NOT work** — compute `sizeof` inside the function:\n\n```c\nvoid f(int *arr) {\n    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);   // WRONG: 8/4 = 2, always\n}\n```\n\nInside the function `arr` is already a pointer, so `sizeof(arr)` is 8 (pointer size), giving you a garbage count every time. That's precisely the warning GCC gave you in lesson5b.\n\nSo your idea is right, with one correction: you can't pass `sizeof(arr)` *from inside* — you compute the length in the caller (where the array type is still known) and pass that number in. Which is exactly what the conventional `int n` parameter *is*:\n\n```c\nint a[10];\nf(a, 10);                          // pass the length directly\nf(a, sizeof(a)/sizeof(a[0]));      // or let sizeof compute it for you — same thing\n```\n\nThe second form is actually the preferred idiom because it stays correct if you change the array size later. But notice: **it still boils down to passing a number**. There's no way to avoid handing the length across the boundary — you're just choosing whether to type `10` yourself or let the compiler calculate it in the one place it still can.\n\nOne subtle gotcha to lock it in:\n\n```c\nint a[10];\nint *p = a;\n// sizeof(a) == 40   (array type, knows the size)\n// sizeof(p) == 8    (pointer, size is gone)\n```\n\nSame data, two names — but `a` has type `int[10]` and `p` has type `int *`. The `sizeof` trick only works on the former. Does that clear it up?",
          "thinking": {
